<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">United States Military Government in Cuba</h1><p class="st-meta">1898 CE – 1902 CE · capital Havana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">American military rule over Cuba after the Spanish-American War, which ended yellow fever in Havana and left the island bound by the Platt Amendment.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> entered the Cuban War of Independence in April 1898, destroyed the Spanish fleet off <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in July and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to give up Cuba in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898">Treaty of Paris</a>. On 1 January 1899 Spain formally handed over the island to an American military government, excluding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-cuba-in-arms">Republic of Cuba in Arms</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The military governors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-r-brooke">John R. Brooke</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-wood">Leonard Wood</a>, ruled by decree. They disbanded the Cuban army, built roads, schools and sewers, and organised elections. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-reed">Walter Reed's</a> commission confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-finlay">Carlos Finlay's</a> theory that mosquitoes carried yellow fever, and the disease was eradicated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>. American investors bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> land and railways. A Cuban convention drafted a constitution in 1900 to 1901.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The convention was forced to adopt the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platt-amendment">Platt Amendment</a>, which gave the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> a right to intervene and a naval base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guantanamo-bay-naval-base">Guantánamo</a>. Power passed to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-estrada-palma">Tomás Estrada Palma</a> on 20 May 1902, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba</a> (1902-1959).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-states-military-government-in-cuba.jpg" alt="United States Military Government in Cuba" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: Jacobolus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_(1896%E2%80%931908).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1898 CE – 1902 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Havana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John R. Brooke, Leonard Wood</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish and English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>US occupation of Cuba (1898-1902)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1900&amp;focus=united-states-military-government-in-cuba" aria-label="United States Military Government in Cuba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2481 1324 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United States Military Government in Cuba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2801 1580L2801 1580M2801 1580L2570 1008M2801 1580L2801 1580"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2801 1580h0M2570 1008h0M2801 1580h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2801 1580h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2801 1580h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Havana. <a href="/atlas?year=1900&amp;focus=united-states-military-government-in-cuba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
